.TH UMOUNT 1 "November 2026" "LikeOS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
umount \- unmount file systems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B umount
.RB [ \-flnrv ]
.IR directory | source ...
.br
.B umount \-a
.RB [ \-flnrv ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR types ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B umount
detaches mounted file systems: what
.BR mount (1)
attached.
Each argument is a mount point, or the source a file system is mounted
from \(em a device
.RB ( /dev/sdb1 )
or a tag naming one
.RB ( UUID= ,
.BR LABEL= ,
.BR PARTUUID= ,
.BR PARTLABEL= ).
When several file systems are mounted on top of each other at one
directory, the topmost is unmounted.
.PP
Before a file system is detached, what is still buffered for it is
written; a file system on which files are open, or which is some process's
working directory, is busy and stays mounted (see
.B \-l
and
.BR \-r ).
.PP
Unmounting needs root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Unmount every file system that can be, the most recently mounted first:
everything but the root and the kernel's own
.RB ( /proc ,
.BR /sys ,
.BR /dev ).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-types " \fIlist\fR"
With
.BR \-a :
only the file systems of these types (separated by commas; a
.B no
prefix excludes a type).
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Force the unmount.
Accepted for compatibility: files open on the file system still keep it
busy.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-lazy
Detach the file system now, and finish unmounting it when the last file
open on it is closed.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-read\-only
When the file system is busy, mount it read-only instead.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-mtab
Accepted; there is no
.B /etc/mtab
to update.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Say what is unmounted.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong usage, or no permission.
.TP
.B 2
A system error.
.TP
.B 32
The unmount failed.
.TP
.B 64
Some of several unmounts failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /proc/mounts
What is mounted now.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
umount /mnt
umount LABEL=STICK
umount \-l /media/stick
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (1),
.BR fstab (5)
